import json
import os

extract_html = r"""<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Kr" class="wiki-link" title="Krypton (Kr)">Krypton</a></b> (symbol <b>Kr</b>, atomic number 36) is a rare, colorless, odorless, and tasteless noble gas located in Group 18 of the periodic table. Named from the Greek word <i>kryptos</i>, meaning "the hidden one," krypton occurs in minute trace amounts—about 1.1 parts per million—in Earth's atmosphere and is extracted commercially through the cryogenic distillation of liquid air. Though famous in popular culture as the fictional weakness of Superman, real-world krypton is a peaceful, chemically aloof gas that glows with a brilliant whitish-green light when electrified. India shares a monumental historical link with krypton through its co-discoverer, Morris Travers, who moved to Bangalore in 1906 to become the founding Director of the Indian Institute of Science (IISc). Today, krypton plays vital roles in high-speed photographic flashes, energy-efficient window insulation, deep-space satellite thrusters, precision lasers, and advanced medical lung imaging.</p>"""

history_html = r"""<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/c804572a9e0b3c0a5cb862b9e53b9076.jpg" decoding="async" width="190" height="291" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.75" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" data-file-width="800" data-file-height="1226" /><figcaption>Sir William Ramsay, the discoverer of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Kr" class="wiki-link" title="Krypton (Kr)">krypton</a></figcaption></figure><p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Kr" class="wiki-link" title="Krypton (Kr)">Krypton</a> was discovered in London in May 1898 by Scottish chemist Sir William Ramsay and English chemist Morris Travers. The duo isolated it while searching for hidden elements in atmospheric air. By chilling samples of air until they turned into a liquid and then carefully evaporating the liquid, they captured the very last droplets of liquid air remaining in the vessel. When they placed this leftover residue into a vacuum tube and applied high voltage, spectroscopic analysis revealed a new, distinct set of bright green and yellow emission lines. Ramsay named the gas <i>krypton</i> from the Greek word <i>kryptos</i>, meaning "hidden." Just weeks later, Ramsay and Travers went on to discover <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">neon</a>, followed by <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Xe" class="wiki-link" title="Xenon (Xe)">xenon</a> in September 1898. Ramsay was awarded the 1904 Nobel Prize in Chemistry for discovering this entire family of noble gases.</p>
<p><b>The Indian Legacy of Co-Discoverer Morris Travers:</b> Krypton's co-discoverer, Morris William Travers—affectionately dubbed "Rare Gas Travers" in European scientific circles—holds a historic connection with India. In 1906, Travers was selected to move to Bangalore as the founding Director of the prestigious <b>Indian Institute of Science (IISc)</b>. Working alongside the vision of pioneer industrialist Jamsetji Tata and the Maharaja of Mysore, Travers spent nearly a decade constructing IISc's iconic main building, establishing its first chemistry and physics laboratories, and laying the foundations for modern scientific research and low-temperature cryogenic physics on the Indian subcontinent.</p>
<p><b>Ancient Philosophical Parallels:</b> The naming of krypton as "the hidden one" echoes ancient Indian philosophical thought. Long before modern chemistry isolated atmospheric gases, classical Indian Vedic and Upanishadic texts (such as the <i>Vaisheshika</i> and <i>Sankhya</i> schools of philosophy) conceptualized invisible, subtle elements—termed <i>Sukshma</i> or <i>Gupta</i> (hidden) entities—that reside within <i>Vayu</i> (air) and <i>Akasha</i> (space). Ancient scholars recognized that atmospheric air was not a simple uniform substance, but contained subtle, imperceptible components that sustained natural order.</p>
<p><b>Redefining the International Standard of Length (Meter):</b> In 1960, krypton made scientific history by defining the exact length of the meter. The International Bureau of Weights and Measures retired the old 1889 physical prototype bar made of platinum-iridium alloy kept in Sèvres, France, and redefined 1 meter as precisely <b>1,650,763.73 wavelengths</b> of the orange-red light emitted by the decay of the isotope <b>krypton-86</b> (<sup>86</sup>Kr) in a vacuum. This atomic standard also replaced the 1927 definition of the angstrom (based on <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cd" class="wiki-link" title="Cadmium (Cd)">cadmium</a>'s red line) with \(1\text{ Å} = 10^{-10}\text{ m}\). In India, the <b>National Physical Laboratory (NPL India)</b> in New Delhi constructed custom interferometric comparators using krypton-86 discharge lamps to maintain India's primary standard of length. The krypton-86 standard remained the international benchmark until October 1983, when the meter was redefined based on the distance light travels in a vacuum in \(1/299,792,458\) of a second.</p>"""

characteristics_html = r"""<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Kr" class="wiki-link" title="Krypton (Kr)">Krypton</a> is a heavy, colorless, odorless, and completely non-toxic noble gas. Under standard atmospheric conditions, krypton exists as single, unattached atoms (a monatomic gas). When excited inside an evacuated gas discharge tube by an electric current, ionized krypton emits a brilliant, multi-hued light discharge that appears bright white or greenish-blue to the human eye. Spectroscopic analysis of krypton reveals several sharp, intense emission lines, with its most prominent spectral signatures located in the green and yellow regions of the visible spectrum.</p>
<p>With a fully filled valence shell electron configuration (\(1s^2 2s^2 2p^6 3s^2 3p^6 4s^2 3d^{10} 4p^6\)), krypton is extremely unreactive. For decades, it was believed to be completely inert. However, under extreme laboratory conditions, krypton can be forced to form chemical compounds. In 1963, chemists synthesized <b>krypton difluoride (KrF<sub>2</sub>)</b>, a colorless crystalline solid that acts as a powerful fluorinating agent. Krypton difluoride can only be stored at sub-zero temperatures, as it spontaneously decomposes into krypton and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/F" class="wiki-link" title="Fluorine (F)">fluorine</a> gas at room temperature. Other exotic species, such as krypton hydride fluoride (HKrF) and inclusion compounds (clathrates) trapped inside ice or organic lattices, have also been observed under cryogenic conditions.</p>
<p>Solid krypton is a white, snow-like substance that crystallizes in a face-centered cubic (fcc) lattice—a crystal structure shared by all noble gases except <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a> (which adopts a hexagonal close-packed structure). Krypton is also produced naturally as a fission fragment when heavy elements like <a href="/school1/ptable/element/U" class="wiki-link" title="Uranium (U)">uranium</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pu" class="wiki-link" title="Plutonium (Pu)">plutonium</a> undergo nuclear fission.</p>
<p><b>Atomic Optics Research in India:</b> In modern Indian experimental physics, krypton plays a central role in quantum state manipulation. At the <b>Raja Ramanna Centre for Advanced Technology (RRCAT)</b> in Indore, scientists at the Atom Optics Laboratory use laser cooling and optical trapping techniques inside a Magneto-Optical Trap (MOT) to slow down metastable krypton atoms using Zeeman slowers. By trapping bosonic isotopes of krypton (\(^{82}\text{Kr}\), \(^{84}\text{Kr}\), and \(^{86}\text{Kr}\)) near absolute zero, Indian researchers conduct fundamental studies in quantum optics, precision atomic spectroscopy, and ultra-cold atomic collisions.</p>"""

physical_properties_html = r"""<p>At standard temperature and pressure (0 °C and 1 atm), elemental <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Kr" class="wiki-link" title="Krypton (Kr)">krypton</a> is a gas with a density of 3.749 g/L—making it about 2.9 times denser than ordinary air (1.29 g/L). Because of its heavy atomic mass (83.798 amu), a vessel filled with krypton feels noticeably heavier than one filled with air or <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a>.</p>
<p>Krypton transitions into a clear, dense liquid at its boiling point of 119.93 K (−153.22 °C) and freezes into a crystalline solid at its melting point of 115.78 K (−157.37 °C). Liquid krypton has a density of approximately 2.41 g/cm³ at its boiling point. The temperature window between liquid krypton's melting and boiling points is exceptionally narrow (only 4.15 K), reflecting the weak van der Waals dispersion forces holding the nonpolar atoms together.</p>
<p>Krypton has a low thermal conductivity of 0.00943 W/(m·K) at room temperature—less than half that of air (0.026 W/(m·K)). This low thermal conductivity makes krypton an extraordinary thermal insulator. Unusually for a gas, sound travels through krypton at only 220 m/s at 20 °C, significantly slower than the speed of sound in air (343 m/s), due to krypton's heavy atomic mass.</p>"""

occurrence_html = r"""<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Kr" class="wiki-link" title="Krypton (Kr)">Krypton</a> is an extraordinarily rare constituent of Earth's atmosphere, making up approximately <b>1.14 parts per million (ppm)</b> by volume (about 1 in 875,000 air molecules) or 3.0 ppm by total mass. Because krypton is highly volatile and non-reactive, it does not remain dissolved in ocean surface waters or bind to minerals in Earth's crust, existing almost entirely as a free gas floating in the atmosphere.</p>
<p>Across the cosmos, krypton is created in massive stars during stellar nucleosynthesis, particularly through the slow neutron capture process (s-process) in giant stars and rapid neutron capture (r-process) during supernovae explosions. Krypton has been detected in interstellar gas clouds, solar wind, and the atmospheres of outer planets like Jupiter, Saturn, and Uranus.</p>
<p><b>Industrial Extraction in India:</b> On Earth, krypton cannot be mined from rocks or gas wells; it is harvested exclusively as a byproduct of industrial <b>cryogenic air separation</b>. Massive air separation plants liquefy atmospheric air by chilling it to sub-zero temperatures below −180 °C, then separate its components through fractional distillation. Because krypton has a higher boiling point (−153.2 °C) than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/N" class="wiki-link" title="Nitrogen (N)">nitrogen</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/O" class="wiki-link" title="Oxygen (O)">oxygen</a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a>, it concentrates alongside <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Xe" class="wiki-link" title="Xenon (Xe)">xenon</a> in the liquid oxygen fraction. In India, major industrial gas suppliers such as <b>INOX Air Products</b> and <b>Linde India</b> operate high-capacity cryogenic air separation units at major industrial hubs—including Hazira (Gujarat), Jamnagar, Rourkela (Odisha), and Bokaro (Jharkhand)—to collect crude rare gas mixtures. These fractions are further distilled and purified to produce ultra-high-purity krypton gas for high-tech semiconductor, lighting, and aerospace manufacturing.</p>"""

isotopes_html = r"""<p>Naturally occurring <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Kr" class="wiki-link" title="Krypton (Kr)">krypton</a> in Earth's atmosphere is composed of six natural isotopes: five completely stable isotopes—<b><sup>80</sup>Kr</b> (2.28%), <b><sup>82</sup>Kr</b> (11.58%), <b><sup>83</sup>Kr</b> (11.49%), <b><sup>84</sup>Kr</b> (57.00%), and <b><sup>86</sup>Kr</b> (17.30%)—plus one observational radioisotope, <b><sup>78</sup>Kr</b> (0.35%). Krypton-78 possesses an extraordinarily long half-life of \(9.2 \times 10^{21}\text{ years}\) (more than 600 billion times the age of the universe!), decaying via double electron capture into selenium-78. This represents the third-longest measured half-life of any radioactive isotope in science, making \(^{78}\text{Kr}\) practically stable for all everyday applications.</p>
<p>In addition to its natural isotopes, nuclear physicists have synthesized over 30 radioactive isotopes and isomers of krypton. Two radioactive isotopes are of immense scientific and environmental importance:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Krypton-81 (<sup>81</sup>Kr):</b> A cosmogenic radioisotope produced in the upper atmosphere when cosmic rays strike krypton-80. It has a half-life of 230,000 years. Because krypton is chemically inert and does not react with rocks, <sup>81</sup>Kr acts as a gold-standard radiometric clock for dating ancient groundwater between 50,000 and 800,000 years old. In India, hydrogeologists at institutions such as the <b>Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC)</b>, the <b>Physical Research Laboratory (PRL, Ahmedabad)</b>, and the <b>National Geophysical Research Institute (NGRI, Hyderabad)</b> track isotope hydrology and Atom Trap Trace Analysis (ATTA) models to evaluate deep paleoclimate aquifers beneath arid regions like Rajasthan's Thar Desert, helping map India's subterranean water security.</li>
<li><b>Krypton-85 (<sup>85</sup>Kr):</b> An inert radioactive noble gas with a half-life of 10.76 years, formed as a fission byproduct when <a href="/school1/ptable/element/U" class="wiki-link" title="Uranium (U)">uranium</a> and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Pu" class="wiki-link" title="Plutonium (Pu)">plutonium</a> atoms split in nuclear reactors and weapons tests. <sup>85</sup>Kr is released into the atmosphere when spent nuclear fuel rods are chopped and dissolved during fuel reprocessing. In India, environmental monitoring teams at <b>BARC</b>, the <b>Indira Gandhi Centre for Atomic Research (IGCAR)</b> in Kalpakkam, and Tarapur monitor atmospheric <sup>85</sup>Kr levels to verify complete fuel dissolution and ensure radiological safety. Internationally, atmospheric <sup>85</sup>Kr levels are 30% higher at the North Pole than at the South Pole due to atmospheric circulation patterns and Northern Hemisphere facilities. Because <sup>85</sup>Kr escapes whenever reactor fuel cladding is opened, global intelligence agencies utilized atmospheric <sup>85</sup>Kr monitoring in the early 2000s to detect clandestine nuclear reprocessing facilities in North Korea and Pakistan.</li>
</ul>"""

applications_html = r"""<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb"><img src="/school1/ptable/theme-assets/default/images/inline/8aa6952edb0fb960f775d5d58d962c9e.jpg" decoding="async" width="250" height="250" class="mw-file-element" data-file-width="2000" data-file-height="2000" /><figcaption><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Kr" class="wiki-link" title="Krypton (Kr)">Krypton</a> gas discharge tube</figcaption></figure><ol>
<li><b>High-Speed Photography &amp; Lighting:</b> When electrified, ionized krypton emits a brilliant white glow with multiple spectral lines. Krypton gas discharge tubes are widely used in high-speed photographic flashes, airport runway strobe lights, and specialized studio lighting. Combined with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Hg" class="wiki-link" title="Mercury (Hg)">mercury</a> vapor, krypton creates luminous advertising signs that glow with an eye-catching greenish-blue hue, while pure krypton provides intense white accents in custom "neon" displays.</li>
<li><b>Energy-Efficient Lighting &amp; Window Insulation:</b> Krypton is mixed with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ar" class="wiki-link" title="Argon (Ar)">argon</a> inside premium fluorescent lamps to lower electrical power consumption, though krypton costs roughly 100 times more than argon. It is also combined with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Xe" class="wiki-link" title="Xenon (Xe)">xenon</a> inside incandescent light bulbs to slow tungsten filament evaporation, allowing higher operating temperatures and brighter light output. Additionally, krypton is injected into the air gap of double and triple-pane insulated glass windows to drastically reduce heat transfer and boost building energy efficiency.</li>
<li><b>High-Power Red &amp; Excimer Lasers:</b> Red krypton-ion lasers produce far higher light output in the red spectrum than standard <a href="/school1/ptable/element/He" class="wiki-link" title="Helium (He)">helium</a>-<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">neon</a> lasers, making multi-watt krypton lasers the gold standard for high-power laser light shows. Furthermore, <b>Krypton Fluoride (KrF) excimer lasers</b> generate short-wavelength deep-ultraviolet light (248 nm) used in advanced nuclear fusion research (inertial confinement fusion) and semiconductor microlithography. As India expands its semiconductor manufacturing footprint under the <b>India Semiconductor Mission</b>, securing high-purity krypton buffer gas for sub-micron chip etching has become a strategic priority.</li>
<li><b>Experimental Particle Physics at CERN:</b> In particle physics, liquid krypton is used to build homogeneous electromagnetic calorimeters to measure particle energies. A famous example is the <b>NA48 experiment at CERN</b> in Geneva, which utilized 27 tonnes of liquid krypton. Krypton's small Molière radius (4.7 cm), short radiation length (\(X_0 = 4.7\text{ cm}\)), and high liquid density (2.4 g/cm³) provide extraordinary spatial resolution for tracking subatomic decays. Indian high-energy physicists from institutions such as <b>Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR)</b>, <b>Variable Energy Cyclotron Centre (VECC, Kolkata)</b>, and <b>Panjab University</b> actively collaborate on CERN experiments utilizing liquid noble gas calorimeters.</li>
<li><b>Medical Imaging &amp; Diagnostic Scans:</b> Hyperpolarized <b>krypton-83 (<sup>83</sup>Kr)</b> is used in Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) to image human lung airways, helping radiologists distinguish between hydrophobic and hydrophilic lung tissue surfaces. In computed tomography (CT) lung ventilation studies, a breathing mixture containing 30% krypton and 30% xenon matches the imaging performance of 40% xenon while avoiding xenon's unwanted anesthetic side effects. In nuclear medicine, the short-lived metastable isotope <b>krypton-81m (<sup>81m</sup>Kr)</b> is inhaled by patients to perform gamma camera lung ventilation/perfusion scans.</li>
<li><b>Spacecraft Electric Propulsion:</b> Krypton is widely used as a propellant for Hall-effect electric ion thrusters on commercial satellites, most notably powering thousands of SpaceX Starlink satellites in low-Earth orbit. Krypton offers a lightweight, highly cost-effective alternative to expensive xenon for orbital maneuver and station-keeping.</li>
</ol>"""

biological_role_html = r"""<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Kr" class="wiki-link" title="Krypton (Kr)">Krypton</a> has no known biological role in any living organism. Because of its complete chemical inertness, it passes through the body without reacting or accumulating in tissues. The trace amounts of krypton present in ambient air are completely harmless to humans, animals, and plants.</p>
<p>However, like all noble gases, if krypton gas accumulates in large quantities in confined, unventilated spaces, it can act as a simple asphyxiant by displacing oxygen from the air. Under high-pressure (hyperbaric) breathing conditions—such as deep-sea diving—krypton exhibits mild narcotic and anesthetic effects similar to nitrogen narcosis, though its low atmospheric abundance makes it impractical for routine medical anesthesia compared to <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Xe" class="wiki-link" title="Xenon (Xe)">xenon</a>.</p>"""

